Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and how they can help  

picking_line  
• picklist_id : joins to picking_list / picking_log if we need the exact‐time sequence (FIFO).  
• order_id : filter to the specific order (421).  
• product_id : identify which product(s) were picked.  
• qty : number of units taken in each pick – will be averaged per product.  

picking_log  
• picklist_id / log_time / activity / pickline_no : if we later want to prove FIFO by the time stamps (oldest “P” activity first). Not strictly required for the average.  

products  
• id / name : converts the product_id we find in picking_line into a readable product name.  

Conditions that will probably be needed  
• picking_line.order_id = 421 → restrict to the wanted sales order.  
• picking_log.activity = 'P' → keep only real “Pick” actions, should we decide to validate FIFO.  
• JOIN products ON products.id = picking_line.product_id → get the name.  
• GROUP BY product_id → one row per product.  
• AVG(qty) AS avg_units → calculate the average quantity picked per product.  

10 exploratory SQL queries (each different, all limited to 20 rows)  
They move from simple inspection to the final answer.

```sql
-- 1. See every line that was picked for order 421
SELECT *
FROM picking_line
WHERE order_id = 421
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Distinct order‑ids present in picking_line (sanity check)
SELECT DISTINCT order_id
FROM picking_line
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Distinct product‑ids that appear in picking lines for order 421
SELECT DISTINCT product_id
FROM picking_line
WHERE order_id = 421
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Join to products to see human names for those product‑ids
SELECT DISTINCT pl.product_id,
       pr.name
FROM picking_line  AS pl
JOIN products      AS pr ON pr.id = pl.product_id
WHERE pl.order_id = 421
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Qty picked in every individual pick‑line for that order (adds line_no)
SELECT pl.picklist_id,
       pl.line_no,
       pl.product_id,
       pl.qty
FROM picking_line AS pl
WHERE pl.order_id = 421
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Total quantity per product for order 421
SELECT pl.product_id,
       SUM(pl.qty) AS total_qty
FROM picking_line AS pl
WHERE pl.order_id = 421
GROUP BY pl.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Average quantity per product for order 421 (what we ultimately need)
SELECT pl.product_id,
       AVG(pl.qty) AS avg_qty
FROM picking_line AS pl
WHERE pl.order_id = 421
GROUP BY pl.product_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Same as #7 but with product names
SELECT pr.name,
       AVG(pl.qty) AS avg_qty
FROM picking_line AS pl
JOIN products     AS pr ON pr.id = pl.product_id
WHERE pl.order_id = 421
GROUP BY pr.name
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. If we want to verify FIFO: look at the pick‑time sequence for the picklist(s) used
SELECT plg.picklist_id,
       plg.log_time,
       plg.pickline_no,
       plg.activity
FROM picking_log AS plg
WHERE plg.picklist_id IN (
        SELECT DISTINCT picklist_id
        FROM picking_line
        WHERE order_id = 421
      )
  AND plg.activity = 'P'
ORDER BY plg.log_time ASC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL: Product name and average number of units picked (FIFO already
-- respected by pick list sequence) for order 421
SELECT pr.name            AS product_name,
       AVG(pl.qty)        AS avg_units_picked
FROM picking_line AS pl
JOIN products    AS pr ON pr.id = pl.product_id
WHERE pl.order_id = 421
GROUP BY pr.name
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries progressively reveal which products were picked for order 421 and culminate in the average quantity per product, consistent with FIFO because the pick lines themselves are recorded in the order in which they were executed.
